# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 A ablation_onset 0.2040 93.759 1.7938e+08 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~94 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
2 - first_light 0.3360 89.661 1.3695e+09 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
3 Y first_yield 2.3550 27.003 1.7481e+13 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 30 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 2.4090 25.336 5.7253e+13 stylized  Light rises x11.6 in 0.16 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 26.9 km (95% of m0); split gen 0 at 24.1 km (90% of m0); yield gen 1 at 24.1 km (34% of m0) +4 more. Dust groups supply 18% of the rise and 17% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 2.4450 24.228 1.1115e+14 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x6.4 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 P peak 2.4476 24.135 1.3418e+15 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+3.4 km from the peak of energy deposition (20.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 31.7 km/s).
7 D rolloff 2.4607 23.749 4.2036e+14 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.01 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 98% of v0, 76% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
8 F flare 2.5295 21.667 2.5999e+14 stylized  Light rises x1.6 in 0.09 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 0 at 24.1 km (90% of m0); yield gen 1 at 24.1 km (34% of m0); yield gen 1 at 22.5 km (16% of m0) +7 more. Dust groups supply 0% of the rise and 6% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
9 T tail 2.8144 14.939 1.3004e+12 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
